+- **Load and the fake mic (2026-11-02, workflow-friction).** In a worktree the three Dictate scripts `dictation-bar`, `refresh` (and so a `run-all --browser` from a worktree) failed with "0 sentences" and looked load-related: `recordings/` is git-ignored, so the worktree has no `jfk.wav`, and the runner handed the scripts that missing path. `TestRunner.wav_path()` now falls back to the main checkout copy. With that fixed, all three passed while two other `--browser` batches (17 scripts) and the whole node suite ran beside them (dictation-bar 30 s, docs-tab 26 s, refresh 126-136 s; their waits are conditions, the fixed `waitForTimeout`s only prove that nothing happens). `refresh` is the slow one (real Whisper, six scenarios), so its runner limit is 300 s, not 180 s. If one of them times out again, read its tail first: the usual cause is the sentence wait ("none in 40 s"), i.e. Whisper on :8178 or the wav, not the page.
 - **Never the live Servex (2026-11-02).** Browser scripts run fenced: `TestRunner.browser()` sets `FENCE_SERVEX=1` and points `PWENTRY` at [playwright-fenced.mjs](../playwright-fenced.mjs), so any request to `127.0.0.1:8090` that a script does not stub itself gets a harmless reply and is listed at exit (`servex fence: kept off the live Servex: …`). Scripts that stub Servex use `route.fallback()`, not `route.continue()`. Why: the Dictate scripts posted made-up sessions to the live `/api/session/say` and one of those ended Servex at 00:31. Detail and test: [Servex/doc/restart.md](/framework/servex/doc/restart.md#when-servex-crashes-what-comes-back-why-and-what-to-watch-2026-11-02).
